---
/* oberrieden.pub/follow -> a choice of Instagram or Facebook.

   The target of the "Follow us" code on the printed banner. The banner once
   pointed at /instagram alone; the pub has had a Facebook page on the site
   since, and a guest should get to pick. /instagram still redirects straight
   through, so anything already printed with it keeps working.

   One page for both languages, because both strips of the banner carry the
   same code. Kept short for the same reason as /review: a short URL makes a
   less dense QR code that scans from further away. If either link changes,
   pub.ts changes and nothing has to be reprinted.

   Deliberately not built on Base.astro: someone arriving from a QR code wants
   two buttons, not the menu, hours and photos. It borrows the site's colours,
   two of its fonts and the same brand marks.

   The landlord at the taps heads the page, in a column with the links below
   him, not behind them. As a background his face ended up under the buttons
   and the photo lost its character. The bottom of the photo fades into the
   maroon so the heading can sit on the fade. At phone width the whole page,
   face included, fits on one screen without scrolling.

   The photo is a cleaned-up version of the original (lens flare off the tap
   badges), 929 px wide: enough for the 480 px column at twice the density.
   His consent covers it; nobody else is in the frame. */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
import { pub } from "../../data/pub";
import landlord from "../../assets/photos/landlord-taps.jpg";
---

<html lang="de-CH">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <title>Big Ben Pub · Folgen Sie uns / Follow us</title>
    <style>
      @font-face {
        font-family: "Bitter";
        src: url("/fonts/bitter-700-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
        font-weight: 700;
        font-display: swap;
      }
      @font-face {
        font-family: "Cabin";
        src: url("/fonts/cabin-600-latin.woff2") format("woff2");
        font-weight: 600;
        font-display: swap;
      }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        min-height: 100vh;
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center;
        align-items: center;
        background: #531c23;
        color: #f0e8dc;
        font: 16px/1.5 system-ui, Arial, sans-serif;
        text-align: center;
      }
      .page { width: 100%; max-width: 480px }
      /* Where the column is the full width of the screen, the photo starts
         at the top edge. Centred, it left a band of maroon above it. */
      @media (max-width: 480px) {
        body { align-items: flex-start }
      }
      /* The photo fades to the page's maroon over its lower part, so the
         heading below can overlap it without a hard edge. */
      .photo { position: relative; margin: 0 }
      .photo :global(img) { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto }
      .photo::after {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        background: linear-gradient(rgba(83,28,35,0) 52%, rgba(83,28,35,.75) 78%, #531c23 100%);
      }
      main {
        position: relative;
        margin: -76px auto 0;
        padding: 0 16px 24px;
        max-width: 360px;
      }
      /* The ask is a small caps line in both languages, the same words as the
         captions under the banner's codes, so neither language outranks the
         other. The heading is the handle, which is the same on Instagram and
         Facebook (pub.ts keeps them in step). */
      .pub {
        margin: 0 0 8px;
        font: 600 13px/1.4 Cabin, system-ui, sans-serif;
        letter-spacing: .18em;
        color: #ddba7a;
      }
      h1 {
        margin: 0 0 22px;
        font: 700 30px/1.25 Bitter, Georgia, serif;
        text-shadow: 0 1px 6px rgba(30,22,17,.6);
      }
      ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 14px }
      a.choice {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 14px;
        padding: 14px 18px;
        border-radius: 12px;
        background: #fbf7f0;
        color: #531c23;
        text-decoration: none;
        font: 600 18px/1.3 Cabin, system-ui, sans-serif;
        text-align: left;
      }
      a.choice:hover, a.choice:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #ddba7a; outline-offset: 2px }
      a.choice small { display: block; font: 400 14px/1.3 system-ui, sans-serif; color: #8a6239 }
      /* The same coloured marks as the site's hero and footer: Instagram's
         gradient tile with the camera in white, Facebook's blue disc with a
         white spot behind the f. */
      a.choice :global(.icon) { flex: none; width: 40px; height: 40px }
      a[href*="instagram.com"] :global(.icon) {
        box-sizing: border-box; padding: 6px; border-radius: 12px; color: #fff;
        background: radial-gradient(circle at 30% 107%,#fdf497 0%,#fdf497 5%,#fd5949 45%,#d6249f 60%,#285aeb 90%);
      }
      a[href*="facebook.com"] :global(.icon) {
        color: #1877f2; background: radial-gradient(circle,#fff 0 55%,transparent 56%);
      }
      /* The website is a quiet line, not a third button: the socials are what
         this page is for, and the site is deliberately static. A gold globe
         beside the address, no box. */
      .site { margin: 24px 0 0; font: 600 16px/1.3 Cabin, system-ui, sans-serif }
      .site a { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; color: #ddba7a }
      .site :global(.icon) { width: 22px; height: 22px; flex: none }

      /* On a wide screen a 480 px column sat lost in a field of maroon. Here
         the photo takes the left half at full height and the links are
         centred in the right half. The photo is portrait (about 0.77), close
         to the shape of half a laptop screen, so little of it is cropped;
         The fade turns sideways, into the maroon.

         The boundary is width alone, 900 px. It was "wider than tall" once,
         and a big browser window that happened to be tall got the phone
         column, lost in the middle of the screen. Below 900 px the column
         is at least half the window, so it does not look lost.

         On a tall window half the width is a narrow strip of a portrait
         photo, so the crop is anchored on his face, about 40% across and
         20% down, not on the middle of the picture. His head spans roughly
         27% to 53% of the width; the narrowest half this rule allows still
         shows 15% to 65%.

         The pair is at most 1280 px wide. Beyond that the photo was being
         blown up past its own pixels, and the links sat in a vast field.
         Either side of it the page is the site's light paper, so on a big
         screen it reads as a framed page, like the main site's own pages.
         It is at most 1000 px tall too, centred: on a tall window the frame
         had become a narrow strip with the photo drawn at 1.65 times its own
         pixels. At 1000 px it is drawn at no more than 0.83. On an ordinary
         laptop the window is shorter than that, so the frame fills the
         height and the paper shows only at the sides. */
      @media (min-width: 900px) {
        body { background: #fbf7f0; align-items: center }
        .page {
          max-width: 1280px;
          height: min(100vh, 1000px);
          /* The links need about 500 px; a shorter window scrolls rather
             than cutting them off. */
          min-height: 520px;
          display: grid;
          grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
          /* One row exactly the frame's height. Left to size itself, the row
             took the photo's natural height and pushed the links below the
             bottom of a short frame. */
          grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr);
          background: #531c23;
          box-shadow: 0 0 32px rgba(30,22,17,.18);
          overflow: hidden;
        }
        .photo { height: 100% }
        .photo :global(img) { height: 100%; object-fit: cover; object-position: 40% 20% }
        .photo::after {
          background: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(83,28,35,0) 72%, #531c23 100%);
        }
        main {
          margin: 0;
          align-self: center;
          justify-self: center;
          width: 100%;
          max-width: 400px;
          padding: 48px 32px;
          box-sizing: border-box;
        }
        .pub { font-size: 14px }
        h1 { font-size: 38px; margin-bottom: 28px }
        ul { gap: 16px }
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="page">
      <figure class="photo">
        <Image
          src={landlord}
          alt="Der Wirt zapft ein Bier im Big Ben Pub."
          widths={[480, 929]}
          sizes="(max-width: 480px) 100vw, (min-width: 1280px) 640px, (min-width: 900px) 50vw, 480px"
          format="avif"
          quality={72}
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
      </figure>
      <main>
        <p class="pub">FOLGEN SIE UNS · <span lang="en">FOLLOW US</span></p>
        <h1>{pub.instagramHandle}</h1>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a class="choice" href={pub.instagram}>
              <Icon name="instagram" />
              <span>Instagram<small>{pub.instagramHandle}</small></span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a class="choice" href={pub.facebook}>
              <Icon name="facebook" />
              <span>Facebook<small>Big Ben Pub Oberrieden</small></span>
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="site"><a href="/"><Icon name="web" />oberrieden.pub</a></p>
      </main>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
